1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:05,920
Hey everyone.  We're going to be taking a look at how we can better align employees with the best interests of the firm.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,080
To do that, we're going to be looking at a centralized and decentralized systems.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,760
Gaming the system and a problem solving framework.

4
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:15,280
Let's jump into it.

5
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:21,440
Meet Julia.  She's a regional operations director for a fast growing gym chain.

6
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:24,320
A few months ago, she rolled out a new incentive plan.

7
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:32,360
Gym managers could earn sizable bonuses, but only only if they cut operating costs by 10% or more.

8
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:35,160
@ first it looks like a win.

9
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,520
Costs dropped and the spreadsheets glowed green.

10
00:00:39,560 --> 00:00:49,080
However, what we found was that managers were skipping necessary repairs, cutting AC usage and reducing towels and other necessary gym items.

11
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:50,840
Soon, what happened?

12
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:52,680
Members began to complain.

13
00:00:53,160 --> 00:00:56,000
Retention fail and complaints rose.

14
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,120
Two gyms shut down due to equipment failures.

15
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:05,600
This is a classic case of misaligning incentives between the principal and the agent.

16
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:09,440
To understand what happened, we need to examine Julia's organizational structure.

17
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:16,720
She runs a decentralized system in this case where local gym managers control their own budgets, scheduling, staffing and maintenance.

18
00:01:17,680 --> 00:01:27,320
We can also define it more generally as a system where those further down the management hierarchy are are more likely to have information first, so they're more informed than their bosses.

19
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:30,600
In theory, decentralization leverages local knowledge.

20
00:01:31,080 --> 00:01:37,000
Here, agents closest to the customer often have better, more timely information than those at the top.

21
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:41,560
But decentralization only works if two conditions are met.

22
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:45,240
Agents must have accurate and relevant information.

23
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:48,960
Here, agents are empowered to make decisions decisions.

24
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:54,600
Therefore, they need accurate and relevant information to make choices that align with the organizational's goals.

25
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:59,920
And two, their incentives must be aligned with the goals of the principal.

26
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,640
In this case, the firm incentives drive behavior.

27
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:09,960
And misaligned incentives may have agents seeking out personal gain as opposed to the best interest of the firm.

28
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:15,320
For example, the gym managers and their actions based on the misaligned incentives given to them.

29
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:18,520
Here they are looking for ways to cut costs.

30
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:21,800
But it comes at the cost of destabilizing their gems.

31
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:30,400
Julia gave Baninger's decision rights but incentivized only cost reduction without having balanced performance measures.

32
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:36,760
Her structure encouraged behaviors that optimize their local metric while undermining firm wide objectives.

33
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:39,080
Then we also have the centralized system.

34
00:02:39,720 --> 00:02:42,680
This type of system places authority on a top down approach.

35
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:45,960
Aligning incentives is still important here as well.

36
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:48,200
And here are a few ways to do that.

37
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:53,080
Let's start with linking performance metrics to system wide outcomes.

38
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:55,480
This is when decisions come from the top.

39
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:59,960
Employees should be rewarded based on how well firm level goals are achieved.

40
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:03,960
Not Just local or team specific outcomes.

41
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:09,560
Next, we can create feedback loops between performance and policy.

42
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:14,120
Frontline workers often implement those policies, but they don't shape them.

43
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:21,160
Incentives should encourage employees to share insights from the field so they can improve centralized decisions.

44
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:25,400
Next, we can reward the how and not just the what.

45
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:29,080
When execution is the employee's main responsibility.

46
00:03:29,720 --> 00:03:39,720
Focus incentives not just on hitting output targets, but how work is performed, especially in customer facing or safety critical roles.

47
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:41,960
Lastly, promote shared success.

48
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:50,200
Use team or organization wide bonuses tied to overall firm profitability, customer satisfaction and safety records.

49
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:57,000
This helps everyone stay invested in the central plan success even if they didn't create it.

50
00:03:57,160 --> 00:03:59,240
Let's continue to move on with our GEM example.

51
00:03:59,560 --> 00:04:02,920
This leads us to the core problem of gaming incentives.

52
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:13,440
When rewards here are tied too tightly to one metric, especially when thresholds are involved, employees begin to manipulate behavior to maximize their personal gain.

53
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:16,880
In Julia's case, cost cutting became the singular goal.

54
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:20,000
So managers did exactly what the system encouraged.

55
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,320
They delayed repairs, reduced cleaning hours and ignored equipment wear.

56
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:28,080
And they got the customer response from that.

57
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:33,360
From an organizational economics perspective, this is a form of moral hazard.

58
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:41,680
Agents take actions that serve their own interest but impose costs on the firm to reduce that gaming.

59
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:43,920
We have a few things that we can do.

60
00:04:44,400 --> 00:04:48,080
First, we can replace hard targets with continuous incentives.

61
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:52,560
Hard targets like hitting $100,000 in sales or you don't get a bonus.

62
00:04:52,960 --> 00:04:55,600
They create sharp cliffs in employee behavior.

63
00:04:55,840 --> 00:05:00,520
People either push too hard just to meet threshold or they give up if they don't.

64
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:07,240
If they think they'll miss it worse when the target is hit, they often coast since there's no more upside.

65
00:05:07,560 --> 00:05:20,200
So if we use continuous or linear incentives where every unit of improvement yields a reward, this encourages sustained effort and minimizes the temptation to just pull or push activity across reporting periods.

66
00:05:20,200 --> 00:05:33,000
As an example, instead of giving a $5,000 bonus for reaching 100,000 of a sales quota, offer 50% for every unit sold, whether it lands or not.

67
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:38,280
Next, we can use multi dimensional performance metrics.

68
00:05:38,520 --> 00:05:43,400
Here, employees are rewarded for a single metric like cost reduction or sales volume.

69
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:50,520
They may hit the number in ways that hurt other areas like quality, customer experience, maybe even ethics.

70
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:57,280
So what we want to do is incorporate multiple performance indicators that reflect reflect the complexity of real world success.

71
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:04,880
So when employees are accountable for multiple outcomes, they are less likely to gain one at the expense of the other.

72
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:21,120
So instead of evaluating a customer rep solely based on call volume, add metrics for resolution, quality, customer satisfaction and complaint follow up make manipulation more costly than compliance.

73
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:31,520
Here, gaming happens when the expected payoff of manipulating the system is greater than the cost, whether in effort, detection, risk or consequences.

74
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:40,160
So what we want to do is design systems where manipulation is harder, riskier or less lucrative than doing the right thing.

75
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:47,520
This might mean audit trails, peer review, or reputational consequences tied to gaming behavior.

76
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:58,960
For example, if a regional manager defers expenses to inflate their quarterly profit, then what we might want to do is tie part of their bonus to accuracy and not just performance.

77
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:05,000
Lastly, track both inputs and outcomes to detect surface level wins.

78
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:12,800
With deeper cost, gaming often looks like a success until we look at some of these deeper metrics involved.

79
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:19,800
For instance, sales may rise while returns spike or cost fall while customer churn increases.

80
00:07:20,360 --> 00:07:25,720
So what we want to do is measure both what employees do and what results from it.

81
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:33,480
This provides a fuller picture and exposes some short termism or behavior that undermines long term value.

82
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:47,000
As an example, if maintenance staff are rewarded for completing work orders, then we might want to track equipment failure and equipment failure rates or repeated service requests.

83
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:52,600
So this pulls us to the three question framework, powerful diagnostic tool for principal agent theory.

84
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:54,360
We have our first question.

85
00:07:54,840 --> 00:07:56,600
Who was making the bad decision?

86
00:07:57,240 --> 00:08:03,320
Well, in this case of our gym managers, they had all the formal authority and so they had all the decision rights.

87
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:09,000
Second question, did they have the right information to make a good decision?

88
00:08:10,240 --> 00:08:14,720
Partially.  They understood their budgets but lacked some system wide visibility.

89
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:19,360
They couldn't see member satisfaction scores or facility risk indicators.

90
00:08:20,640 --> 00:08:23,800
Were their incentives aligned with the firm's objectives?  No.

91
00:08:23,800 --> 00:08:30,880
Their compensation was linked narrowly to short term cost control, not long term customer retention or brand equity.

92
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:35,520
So in economic terms, this is a classic principal agent problem.

93
00:08:35,600 --> 00:08:44,040
Agents are optimizing their own utility function and complete contracts in a system where residual risk is borne by the firm.

94
00:08:44,600 --> 00:08:46,920
So how can Julia fix it?

95
00:08:47,480 --> 00:09:05,400
Well, she can adjust the incentive structure to reward multidimensional performance, improve internal reporting to obtain real time feedback, reassign high risk decisions to a centralized coordinator, and then tie bonuses to shared metrics and not isolated targets.

96
00:09:05,640 --> 00:09:08,280
Fixing performance starts with fixing the system.

97
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:11,480
Bad outcomes don't always mean bad people.

98
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:12,680
It's just bad design.

99
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:17,160
Julia looked at the incentives that were in place and realized that they didn't work.

100
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:24,920
By focusing on the three questions, Julia was able to reconsider how to align employees to the firm's best interest.

101
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:33,920
Creating the proper incentives allow the gyms to improve retention satisfaction and therefore raising overall profit level.

102
00:09:34,320 --> 00:09:43,840
We can conclude by saying that when people have the right information, the right authority and the right incentives, they don't just perform at a personal level.

103
00:09:43,920 --> 00:09:46,320
They perform in the firm's best interest.

104
00:09:47,040 --> 00:09:48,080
And that's it.

105
00:09:48,160 --> 00:09:49,280
Hope you've enjoyed it.